<body>

<h1>ARGUS &mdash; How the System Works</h1>
<p class="sub">National DNS Caching-Server Health &amp; Cache-Poisoning Monitoring System</p>

<h2>1. What ARGUS does</h2>
<p>Every ISP and organisation runs a <b>caching DNS resolver</b>. A cache can be
<b>poisoned</b> &mdash; an attacker plants a wrong address in it, so everyone
using that resolver is sent to the wrong place. ARGUS is a monitoring system
that watches these resolvers, from one central machine, every minute, and
reports whether any of them is serving a hijacked answer.</p>
<p>ARGUS is <b>not</b> a DNS server. It holds no cache and has no users &mdash; it
is a measuring instrument pointed at other resolvers.</p>

<h2>2. How it detects a hijack &mdash; the two paths</h2>
<p>For each domain, ARGUS resolves the same name twice and compares:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Path</th><th>Command</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Untrusted</b></td><td><code>dig @&lt;resolver&gt; &lt;domain&gt;</code></td>
      <td>What the monitored cache claims. Never trusted.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Trusted</b></td><td><code>dig +trace &lt;domain&gt;</code></td>
      <td>Walked from the root &rarr; TLD &rarr; authoritative server. This is
          the true answer (ground truth).</td></tr>
</table>
<p>ARGUS compares the two answers <b>as sets of IP addresses</b> (a domain may
have several addresses for load balancing, which is normal).</p>

<h2>3. The four possible results</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>MATCH</b></td><td>The resolver's addresses equal the trusted answer. Healthy.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>PARTIAL</b></td><td>A subset &mdash; usually load balancing, not an attack.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>MISMATCH</b></td><td>The resolver returned an address the zone never published &mdash; the fingerprint of poisoning.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>ERROR</b></td><td>The resolver did not answer (timeout / unreachable). Not a hijack.</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="note"><b>Careful terminology:</b> ARGUS never calls a mismatch
"confirmed poisoning". A difference is investigated through six checks (a second
independent walk, cross-check resolvers, DNSSEC, and repetition) before it is
reported as <b>POSSIBLE cache poisoning</b> &mdash; possible, never proven,
because a passive observer cannot obtain proof.</p>

<h2>4. How it runs &mdash; centralised, every minute</h2>
<p>One central machine runs a <b>sweep</b> every 60 seconds: for each enabled
resolver and each watched domain, it performs the two paths above, compares
them, stores the result, and raises an alert if the evidence supports one. All
results are written to one local database (SQLite).</p>

<h2>5. The nine pages</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Page</th><th>Shows</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Dashboard</td><td>Overall status: resolvers, domains, findings, response-time chart, national map, schedule.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Resolvers</td><td>Health of each monitored resolver (verified vs candidate).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Domains</td><td>The watch-list and how each domain behaved.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monitoring</td><td>Run a live check on one domain &mdash; the two paths side by side.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Results</td><td>Every measurement, searchable, with MATCH / MISMATCH / ERROR.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Alerts</td><td>Findings by severity, with the full evidence for each.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reports</td><td>Generate PDF, CSV or Excel reports from real data.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Settings</td><td>The configuration in force.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Help</td><td>How detection works, in plain terms.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>All nine pages read from the <b>same database</b>. A check made by the engine,
or run by hand on the Monitoring page, immediately appears on Results, the
Dashboard, Alerts and Reports.</p>

<h2>6. How to start it</h2>
<p>Two programs share one database: one collects data, one shows it.</p>
<p><b>Collect data continuously (every minute):</b></p>
<div class="cmd">.venv/bin/python -m argus serve</div>
<p><b>Show the website</b> (in a second terminal), then open a browser at
<code>127.0.0.1:8080</code>:</p>
<div class="cmd">.venv/bin/python -m argus dashboard</div>
<p><b>Run one check immediately</b> (no waiting):</p>
<div class="cmd">.venv/bin/python -m argus run-once</div>

<h2>7. Current status</h2>
<ul>
  <li>The engine, the two-path comparison and the nine-page interface are built and working.</li>
  <li>It monitors 5 real global resolvers (Google, Cloudflare, Quad9, OpenDNS, Verisign) plus <b>10 real Sri Lankan resolver candidates</b> (SLT, Dialog, Mobitel, Lanka Communication Services).</li>
  <li>The Sri Lankan resolvers are marked <b>candidate</b> (from a public directory) until confirmed reachable from the monitoring machine, at which point they become <b>verified</b>.</li>
  <li>Planned next steps: query every authoritative nameserver for agreement, and DNSSEC-validate the trusted walk itself &mdash; the "sophistication" hardening.</li>
</ul>

<p class="foot">ARGUS v1.0 &middot; Department of Computer Science, Sri Lanka.
All figures on the dashboard are real measurements from the system's own
database; no data is fabricated. &ldquo;Monitor the DNS. Secure the future.&rdquo;</p>

</body>
</html>
